-- title:
--   Walsh norm bounds pass from finite-rank to compact operators
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that a real Banach space $X$ with the approximation property is K-convex (the Rademacher projection is bounded on $L_2(\{\pm1\}^n;X)$ uniformly in $n$) if and only if $X$ and its dual $X^*$ both have finite cotype. The converse direction shows that such $X$ has nontrivial type, i.e. does not contain $\ell_1^n$ uniformly, and then derives K-convexity from a power-gap estimate for commuting coordinate projections. A normed space $Z$ satisfies the fixed cotype bound of length $m$ with constant $D$ if $\bigl(\sum_{i\le m}\|z_i\|^2\bigr)^{1/2}\le D\bigl(\mathbb E\|\sum_{i\le m}\varepsilon_iz_i\|^2\bigr)^{1/2}$ for all $z_1,\dots,z_m\in Z$. The Walsh norm $\omega_n(A)$ of an operator $A$ is the norm of $f\mapsto(v\mapsto\mathbb E_u\chi_v(u)Af(u))$ on $L_2(\{0,1\}^n)$-valued functions. A Banach space $X$ has the approximation property if on every compact set the identity can be uniformly approximated by finite-rank operators. Let $X$ have the approximation property, $G$ be a real normed space, $J:G\to X$ a compact operator, $n\in\mathbb N$ and $\rho\ge0$. Suppose every finite-rank operator $A:G\to X$ satisfies $\omega_n(A)\le\rho\|A\|$. Then
--   $$
--   \omega_n(J)\le\rho\,\|J\| .
--   $$
--
--   The approximation property lets estimates for finite-rank operators reach compact ones.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/Analysis/Cotype/WalshMainEstimates.lean#L39-L58

